-- allium: 3
-- board-layout.allium
--
-- Scope: The board's structural layout — which navigation column and which
--        section a card lands in, how the cursor moves through them, the
--        flattened rendering mode, the vertical bands of the board frame,
--        the board lens, and the filters that narrow what a column renders.
-- Includes: Board Columns (the navigation index space, review-section order,
--           row navigation and the `gg` chord), the board's view
--           (BoardView: visibility, epic card placement, flattening, column
--           sections and the epic tree), EpicGroupTree (and the epic indent
--           config), collapsed sections
--           (SectionRef, CollapsedSections), epic folding
--           (EpicFoldRef, FoldedEpics), board vertical layout, the
--           board lens (BoardLens: the only-active filter and flattening),
--           hidden epics and work packages (BoardHides), the board-filter
--           overlay layout, the board search filter, cursor selection preservation, and the shared
--           single-line text buffer the input panel edits (TextInputField).
-- Excludes: The board's visual language — identity hue, the focus channel,
--           header-bar and card chrome, the neutral ground ramp (see
--           board-visuals.allium, "Column Identity and Focus"); the domain model the
--           layout is keyed on — TaskStatus, SubStatus, ColumnSection (see
--           core.allium); the rule that folds a section or an epic group
--           (see tasks.allium: ToggleSectionCollapse, ToggleEpicFold); epic
--           substatus (see epics.allium).

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./work-packages.allium" as wp
use "./settings.allium" as settings

-- The board's one view: what the operator is looking at, and the lens and
-- hide-lists that narrow it. See "Board View".
given {
    board_view: BoardView
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Board Columns
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The kanban board renders exactly four task-status columns — Backlog,
-- Running, Review, Done — and nothing else. A task card's column
-- is decided by task.status alone. Sub-status is NOT a column axis: it groups
-- cards into sections *within* a column, under a header naming the section
-- (see core.allium, "Column Sections", and "Column Section Layout" below).
--
-- An epic card is not placed that way, and is not placed once: it appears in
-- every column where the epic's subtree has visible work, so one epic can hold
-- a card in all four at the same time. See "Epic Card Placement" below.
--
-- Navigation column indices are 1-based: 1–4, the h/l cursor positions.
-- Index 0 is not a column at all, and handlers handed it return without
-- acting.
--
-- There is one column index space and it is the navigation one. There are
-- no sub-columns per (status, sub_status) group: sub-status structure is shown
-- by ColumnSection sections inside the column, which also fold.
--
-- Review-column section order (within nav column 3, top to bottom). Sections are
-- ordered by how much they need the user, most first. Three are derived rather
-- than stored — see core.allium, "Derived review sections" under SubStatus:
--
--   # | Section                            | Means
--   --+------------------------------------+------------------------------------
--   1 | conflict                           | rebase/merge conflict — blocked
--   2 | pr closed                          | closed without merging — decide
--   3 | pr unreachable                     | PR cannot be read — fix access or url
--   4 | changes requested                  | on the user's own PR — must fix
--   5 | approved                           | own PR, ready to merge
--   6 | awaiting review                    | nothing to do yet
--   7 | parked                   (derived) | no PR, agent session ended
--   8 | changes requested by me  (derived) | waiting on the other author
--   9 | approved by me           (derived) | nothing left for the user to do
--
-- "pr unreachable" sorts above "changes requested" because it is worse than a
-- known task: until it is fixed the card's review state is not merely
-- unfinished, it is unknown, and the board is quietly reporting a decision that
-- may already have changed.
--
-- "approved" deliberately sorts ABOVE "awaiting review": an approved PR is
-- actionable, an undecided one is not. "approved by me" sorts BELOW every other
-- section for the mirror-image reason: the user already gave the only decision
-- that was theirs to give, so the task needs them less than anything else in the
-- column.
--
-- Navigation column layout (h/l cursor, left to right):
--
--   nav# | Label     | kind        | sections in the column
--   -----+-----------+-------------+----------------------------------------------
--      1 | Backlog   | task-status | none — every card holds sub_status = none
--      2 | Running   | task-status | conflict, crashed, stale, needs input, active
--      3 | Review    | task-status | the nine above, in that order
--      4 | Done      | task-status | none — every card holds sub_status = none
--
-- There is no edge column and no archived status. A finished task stays in
-- Done until the user deletes it ('x' on a Done card — tasks.allium:
-- DeleteKeyRouting), so there is nothing for a fifth column to show.
--
-- Navigation:
--   - h/l (or Left/Right) moves the cursor between nav indices 1–4, on the
--     main board and in ViewMode::Epic alike.
--   - At nav index 1 (Backlog), h/Left is a no-op (already at leftmost edge).
--   - At nav index 4 (Done), l/Right is a no-op (already at rightmost edge).
--   - A task card's column is determined by task.status alone; which section
--     it sits in within that column is BoardView.section_of. An epic
--     card's columns come from BoardView.appears_in instead.
--   - Switching into a different, non-empty navigation column (via h/l or
--     Left/Right) always places the cursor on the first card (row 0) —
--     never a row remembered from a previous visit to that column — and
--     releases the select-all cursor position (on_select_all), which is a
--     cursor move and not a change to the multi-selection: the selected
--     tasks and epics are untouched (see tasks.allium: "Multi-Selection").
--     The column also scrolls back to the
--     top so the first card is visible. A clamped no-op (already at the
--     leftmost/rightmost edge) leaves the cursor and scroll position
--     untouched. Switching into an empty column is also a no-op for the
--     cursor/toggle state, matching the [ / ] jump-to-first/last no-op on
--     an empty column.
--
-- Row navigation (cursor movement within the current column):
--   - j/k (or Down/Up) move the cursor one card at a time within the
--     currently-focused navigation column.
--   - Jump-to-top: moves the cursor to the first card (row 0) of the
--     current column. Bound to `[` and to the vim-style `gg` chord.
--   - Jump-to-bottom: moves the cursor to the last card of the current
--     column. Bound to `]` and to `G` (Shift+G).
--   - `gg`/`G` are pure aliases of `[`/`]`: they carry no new semantics.
--     They share the same behaviour on an empty column (a no-op that
--     leaves cursor/scroll/toggle untouched), the same release of the
--     select-all cursor position on a real move, and the same behaviour in
--     ViewMode::Epic. Jump-to-top/bottom are available on the main board
--     and in epic view.
--   - G/gg are deliberately scoped to the main board and epic view only.
--     Overlays keep their own key handling; this navigation does not
--     touch it.
--
-- The `gg` chord:
--   On the main board (Normal mode) and in epic view, `gg` is a pure
--   chord with no other meaning attached to a lone `g`:
--   - Pressing `g` with no chord pending starts a pending-chord window
--     (records the press time). Nothing else happens yet.
--   - If the next keypress is another `g` within core/config.gg_chord_timeout,
--     the chord completes as jump-to-top.
--   - If the next keypress is anything else — a different key, or a `g`
--     after the window has elapsed — the pending chord is simply
--     abandoned (no action fires for the lone `g`), then the new keypress
--     is processed normally.
--   - If the user goes idle after a lone `g` (no further keypress), the
--     periodic tick handler clears the stale pending chord once it is
--     older than core/config.gg_chord_timeout. Nothing fires; this is cleanup
--     only, not a deferred action.
--   - A pending chord is discarded (dropped, not fired) if the next input
--     transitions away from main-board Normal mode entirely (into the
--     TaskDetail overlay) before it
--     resolves. This is a defensive simplification to stop a stale chord
--     leaking across an unrelated view transition; it is not expected to
--     be user-visible.
--   - `G` (jump-to-bottom) has no such conflict and no chord: wherever it
--     is bound it is a direct alias for `]`. It is bound on the main board
--     and in epic view.
--
-- `space` (jump to the selected task's tmux window, or enter the
-- selected epic):
--   On the main board (Normal mode) and in epic view, a single `space`
--   press fires this action immediately — no chord, no deferred
--   resolution, no latency. It is not on `g`: a lone `g` there would have
--   to wait out the `gg` chord window. Multi-select is on `v`.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Board View
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The board's view: what the operator is looking at, and the controls that
-- narrow it. There is exactly one, and it lives in the TUI (store-split S1):
-- no MCP tool reads it, because no agent sees the operator's board.
--
-- Every derivation below is a rendered answer, never a stored one. No task
-- or epic row records which columns its card appears in, which section it
-- sits in or how deep its epic header is indented, so moving a task between
-- columns moves every card that depends on it without a write.

entity BoardView {
    -- The rows this board holds: the shared rows it subscribes to.
    tasks: Set<core/Task>
    epics: Set<core/Epic>
    packages: Set<wp/WorkPackage>
    -- The repo paths the repo filter admits, with its include/exclude mode
    -- and the empty selection (which admits every repo) already resolved.
    active_repo_filter: Set<String>
    -- The main board, rather than an epic view or a package view.
    is_board: Boolean
    -- The epic an epic view shows. Null on the main board and in a package
    -- view.
    current_epic: core/Epic?
    -- The board search query, "" when none is active ("Board Search
    -- Filter").
    search_query: String
    -- How the board is read ("Board Lens").
    lens: BoardLens
    -- Which epics and packages the operator has hidden ("Hidden Epics and
    -- Work Packages").
    hides: BoardHides

    -- == Visibility ==

    -- The search half of visibility. own_match is board_search_filter's
    -- per-item predicate: a title subsequence match or an id-prefix match.
    matches_search(title, id): search_query = "" or own_match(title, id, search_query)

    -- The four predicates every task card on the board is held to: the repo
    -- filter, the epic hide-list, the only-active filter and the search
    -- query. Flattening is not among them: it decides which column shows a
    -- task (in_scope), not whether the board is hiding it.
    task_is_visible(task):
        task.repo_path in active_repo_filter
        and not hides.task_is_hidden(task)
        and (not lens.only_active or task.current_window != null)
        and matches_search(task.title, task.id)

    -- The tasks under an epic that the epic hide-list leaves on the board.
    unhidden_subtree_tasks(epic):
        filter(epic.subtree_tasks, t => not hides.task_is_hidden(t))

    -- The repo filter and the only-active filter as they apply to an epic
    -- card, read off its unhidden subtree. An epic with no task there passes
    -- the repo filter, so a new, empty epic stays reachable; under the
    -- only-active filter it needs a task with a live session.
    epic_admitted(epic):
        (unhidden_subtree_tasks(epic).count = 0
            or unhidden_subtree_tasks(epic).any(t => t.repo_path in active_repo_filter))
        and (not lens.only_active
            or unhidden_subtree_tasks(epic).any(t => t.current_window != null))

    -- The search half for an epic card ("Board Search Filter"): the epic
    -- matches itself, a sub-epic the board would show matches, or a visible
    -- subtree task does (placing_tasks already holds a task to the query).
    epic_matches_search(epic):
        search_query = ""
        or own_match(epic.title, epic.id, search_query)
        or epic.subtree_epics.any(e =>
            not hides.epic_is_hidden(e) and epic_admitted(e)
            and own_match(e.title, e.id, search_query))
        or has_placing_tasks(epic)

    -- Whether an epic card is drawn at all. Which columns it is drawn in is
    -- appears_in.
    epic_is_visible(epic):
        not hides.epic_is_hidden(epic)
        and epic_admitted(epic)
        and epic_matches_search(epic)

    -- == Epic card placement ("Epic Card Placement") ==

    -- The tasks that place an epic's cards: its whole subtree, held to the
    -- visibility every card on the board is held to.
    placing_tasks(epic): filter(epic.subtree_tasks, t => task_is_visible(t))

    has_placing_tasks(epic): placing_tasks(epic).count > 0

    -- A column draws the epic's card when some placing task holds its
    -- status. An epic with no placing task at all falls back to Backlog, so
    -- it stays reachable. Only a column that renders epic cards draws it.
    appears_in(epic, status):
        placing_tasks(epic).any(t => t.status = status)
        or (not has_placing_tasks(epic) and status = backlog)

    -- The placing tasks one column's copy is derived from. Its section and
    -- its substatus label come from these and nothing else: the epic
    -- substatus (epics.allium, "Epic substatus") computed over this slice,
    -- mapped as in section_of's note below.
    placing_tasks_in(epic, status): filter(placing_tasks(epic), t => t.status = status)

    -- The key an epic's card sorts by in the Done column, read descending
    -- ("Done Column Ordering"). The epic's own completion time wins when it
    -- has one; otherwise the most recent completion in the column's own
    -- slice of the subtree. Null when neither exists, and a null key sorts
    -- last.
    done_sort_key(epic):
        epic.completed_at ?? latest_completion(placing_tasks_in(epic, done))

    -- == Flattening ("Flattened Mode") ==

    -- The column flattening never reaches. The backlog is read at the epic
    -- level, so it keeps its epic cards and every descendant stays inside
    -- the epic it belongs to.
    unflattened_statuses: { backlog }

    -- Flattening is decided per column, never once for the board. A column
    -- is its task status (see "Board Columns").
    flattens(status): lens.flattened and status not in unflattened_statuses

    -- A column renders epic cards unless it is being flattened.
    renders_epics(status): not flattens(status)

    -- What hierarchical mode shows in a column: on the main board, tasks
    -- with no epic; in an epic view, the viewed epic's own direct tasks.
    -- Deeper descendants stay inside their sub-epic's card.
    directly_under(task):
        (is_board and task.epic = null)
        or (current_epic != null and task.epic = current_epic)

    -- Whether a task card is drawn in its status column, on the main board
    -- or in an epic view: a flattened column admits the whole subtree in
    -- view, an unflattened one only what hierarchical mode shows there. A
    -- package view's cards are work-packages.allium's PackageViewCards.
    in_scope(task, status):
        task_is_visible(task)
        and (current_epic = null or task in current_epic.subtree_tasks)
        and (flattens(status) or directly_under(task))

    -- The task cards a column draws.
    column_tasks(status): filter(tasks, t => t.status = status and in_scope(t, status))

    -- == Column sections ("Column Section Layout") ==

    -- The columns that have sections at all. Every other column lists its
    -- cards without headers: its tasks hold sub_status = none throughout.
    -- Unrelated to unflattened_statuses: one is about epic grouping, the
    -- other about section grouping.
    sectioned_statuses: { running, review }

    has_sections(status): status in sectioned_statuses

    -- The section a task card sits in, or null in a column with no
    -- sections. A derived section wins over the task's own sub_status, and
    -- parked is tested first: "no PR at all" dominates any review decision
    -- recorded without one.
    --
    -- An epic card lands in a section too, off its column's display
    -- substatus: "N blocked" -> needs_input, active -> active, in review ->
    -- awaiting_review.
    section_of(task):
        if task.status = review and task.worktree != null
            and task.current_window = null
            and not (task.url != null and task.url.url_type = pr): parked
        else if task.is_review_tagged
            and task.sub_status = changes_requested: changes_requested_by_me
        else if task.is_review_tagged
            and task.sub_status = approved: approved_by_me
        else if task.sub_status = none: null
        else: task.sub_status

    -- Where a flattened column draws epic-header rows: a flattened column
    -- with sections. The flattened Done column has none ("Done Column
    -- Ordering"), so it draws no header and has no epic group to fold.
    draws_epic_headers(status): flattens(status) and has_sections(status)

    -- == The epic tree ("Flattened Mode", Rendering) ==

    section_tasks(status, section):
        filter(column_tasks(status), t => section_of(t) = section)

    -- The compact epic tree one section of a flattened column lays its
    -- groups out as. The tree's top is the viewed epic in an epic view and
    -- the root epics elsewhere; its owners are the epics owning a task in
    -- the section. Folding is not consulted ("Epic Folding").
    epic_tree(status, section):
        EpicGroupTree(top: current_epic, owners: epics_of(section_tasks(status, section)))

    epic_depth(epic, status, section): epic_tree(status, section).depth(epic)

    epic_indent(epic, status, section): epic_tree(status, section).indent(epic)

    -- == Status bar badges ("Board Lens", "Hidden Epics and Work Packages") ==

    -- The hides the badge and the overlay's tab counts name: only epics and
    -- packages the board holds, since the user could never find the row to
    -- unhide a ghost. An epic hidden only through its parent is not counted.
    hidden_epic_count: filter(epics, e => e in hides.hidden_epics).count
    hidden_package_count: filter(packages, p => hides.package_is_hidden(p)).count

    @guidance
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- own_match(title, id, query): board_search_filter's predicate.
        -- latest_completion(tasks): the greatest completed_at among the
        -- tasks, null when none has one.
        -- epics_of(tasks): the epics the tasks name directly, a task with
        -- no epic adding none.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Epic Card Placement
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A task card sits in exactly one column. An epic card sits in every column
-- where the epic has work.
--
-- Intent: an epic is not in one state, it is a spread of states. Placing its
-- card by the epic's own recorded status picked one of them and hid the rest,
-- so an epic with three tasks awaiting review read as "running" and the Review
-- column gave no sign that the epic had anything waiting there. The board's job
-- is to answer "what is in review right now", and an epic whose review work is
-- invisible there is a wrong answer. Drawing the card once per column it has
-- work in puts the spread itself on screen, and the columns an epic is absent
-- from become as informative as the ones it fills.
--
-- Which columns:
--   - A column renders an epic's card when at least one visible task in the
--     epic's subtree — the epic's own tasks and those of every descendant
--     sub-epic, recursively — holds that column's status. Subtree, not direct
--     tasks: the epic card already reports its whole subtree's progress (see
--     epics.allium, "Epic progress"), and a parent whose work all sits one
--     level down would otherwise place no card at all.
--   - Visible means the task survives the same predicates every other card on
--     the board is held to: admitted by the repo filter, the
--     only-active filter and the search query, and not under a hidden epic
--     (see "Hidden Epics and Work Packages"). A task the board is hiding
--     cannot place its ancestor's card — the same reasoning that gates the
--     descendant clause in board_search_filter, and for the same reason:
--     entering the card would be a dead end, because the work that justified
--     it is not there to be found.
--   - The epic's own recorded status places no card. That status is a summary
--     of the very same tasks (see epics.allium, EpicStatusRecalculation), so a
--     column drawn from it would either repeat one the tasks already earned or
--     contradict them.
--   - An epic with no visible task anywhere in its subtree draws its card in
--     Backlog, whatever its recorded status. A freshly created epic is empty by
--     definition, and an epic that vanishes until someone gives it a task
--     cannot be given one.
--     The fallback places a card the board draws at all, and that is a
--     separate question (BoardView.epic_is_visible): the operator's own
--     narrowing still applies to the card itself. A hidden epic draws none,
--     an epic whose every task sits in a repo the filter excludes draws none,
--     and under the only-active filter an epic draws none without a live
--     task. An empty epic passes the repo filter, so it stays reachable. An
--     epic the user is finished with is deleted (epics.allium: DeleteEpic),
--     not kept in a hidden state.
--   - The rule is the same for a sub-epic card inside an epic view: it appears
--     in every column where its own subtree has visible work.
--   - It reaches only the columns that render epic cards at all (see
--     BoardView.renders_epics). A flattened column surfaces the descendant
--     tasks themselves and draws no epic card, so nothing here applies there.
--
-- What each copy shows:
--   - Every copy of one epic's card carries the same progress counts: the whole
--     subtree, not the slice sitting in that column. The card is one epic seen
--     from several places, and counts that changed per column would read as
--     several epics.
--   - The section a copy lands in is derived from that column's own visible
--     subtree tasks, not from the epic's board-wide substatus. A copy in Review
--     sits under the section its review tasks earn; a blocked task over in
--     Running has no say over it. The substatus-to-section mapping is unchanged
--     (see "Column Section Layout").
--   - The substatus label the card carries is derived the same per-column way,
--     for the same reason the section is: a card in the Running column labelled
--     "done" would contradict the column it is sitting in. The label follows the
--     copy; the counts do not.
--
-- Interaction:
--   - Every copy is a full epic card: selectable, and entering any of them
--     opens the same epic view. Nothing tells the copies apart but the column
--     they are in.
--   - Cursor position is per column, as it is for every other card, so the
--     cursor's memory of "the epic card in Review" and "the epic card in
--     Running" are two independent positions.


------------------------------------------------------------
-- Done Column Ordering
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The Done column is read newest-first: the thing that finished a minute ago
-- is the thing the user is looking for, and yesterday's work belongs below it.
-- Every transition into done stamps the card with core/Task.completed_at (or
-- core/Epic.completed_at) — a real wallclock timestamp in milliseconds, not a
-- rank — and the column sorts on it DESCENDING.
--
-- The field is the Done column's own. It replaced a completion rank written
-- into sort_order: the negated timestamp, folded into one shared namespace
-- with manual and feed ordering. Two orderings were wrong for as long as that
-- lasted, and both are fixed by the split rather than worked around:
--   - A feed setting a POSITIVE sort_order on a done task as ordinary manual
--     ordering was indistinguishable from a rank, and sank the card below
--     every genuinely ranked one. Nothing a feed writes reaches completed_at.
--   - A task created directly IN done — a feed upsert whose item arrives with
--     status done — never passed through the transition that stamped the
--     rank, so it carried none. The feed insert stamps completed_at itself
--     (feeds.allium, UpsertFeedTasks).
-- And sort_order means one thing again: no status transition reads or writes
-- it, in either direction.
--
-- An epic card in Done need not be a done epic. It is drawn there because part
-- of its subtree finished (see "Epic Card Placement"). So in the Done column,
-- and only there, an epic card sorts by BoardView.done_sort_key: its
-- own completed_at when it has one, else the freshest completion among the
-- tasks that placed it in this column. A still-running epic takes the second
-- branch and sits next to the work that put it there. A finished epic takes
-- the first, and is dated by its own completion.
--
-- Scope, deliberately narrow:
--   - Done only. Backlog, Running and Review keep the generic
--     `sort_order ?? id` key: those columns carry manual ordering and sections,
--     and nothing is stamped on the way into them.
--   - The subtree slice is the column's own (placing_tasks_in), not the whole
--     subtree. A card in Done ordered by a task that finished elsewhere would
--     be ordered by work the column is not showing.
--   - Only tasks feed the derived half of the key. A done SUB-epic's own
--     completed_at is not consulted: the sub-epic earned it from the very
--     tasks already counted here, so reading both would double-count without
--     adding a card the walk had missed.
--   - Sections come first, as in every column. The key orders cards within a
--     section run; it never moves a card across one.
--
-- In a FLATTENED Done column the epic draws no card at all (see
-- "Flattened Mode"), and no epic header either: a header is emitted inside a
-- section run and Done has no sections. The grouping survives as card order
-- alone — an epic's tasks stay adjacent — and that group's position follows the
-- same principle, for the same reason: the group holding the freshest task is
-- the group the user wants at the top.
--
-- An epicless task in a flattened Done column is a one-card group, dated by
-- its own completed_at alongside the epic groups. Elsewhere in a flattened
-- column such tasks sort last, behind an OrphanSeparator; Done has no sections,
-- so it draws neither header nor separator, and the split that the ordering
-- exists to introduce is not there to be introduced. Sinking a task that
-- finished a minute ago below every epic group would be the same defect this
-- section exists to fix.
--
-- It is NOT BoardView.done_sort_key, though. A flattened group is keyed
-- on a task's DIRECT epic, so its key is the freshest completion among the
-- column's tasks that name that epic — never a descendant's, and never the
-- epic's own completed_at. A sub-epic's tasks form their own group with their
-- own key, so a parent ranked over the whole subtree would claim a position
-- earned by a group sitting elsewhere in the column. The subtree-wide
-- done_sort_key is the hierarchical path's answer, where the epic draws one
-- card standing in for the whole subtree.
--
-- Manual reorder in Done. Moving a card by hand swaps the two cards'
-- completed_at values and persists them (tasks.allium, ReorderItem). This
-- works for a TASK card and for an EPIC card alike: both now own the field the
-- column reads. The epic case used to be refused outright, because the card's
-- key was derived from its subtasks and it owned nothing the column read, so
-- the swap would have persisted a value nothing rendered and the card would
-- have sat exactly where it was. Now the write lands on
-- core/Epic.completed_at, done_sort_key prefers it over the derived subtask
-- key, and the card moves.
--
-- Two costs, both accepted. The swap rewrites a factual timestamp, so after a
-- reorder the field no longer says when the work finished — that is the user
-- explicitly overriding the recorded order, which is the only thing a manual
-- reorder can mean here. And an epic's override is not permanent:
-- EpicStatusRecalculation (epics.allium) rewrites epic.completed_at when the
-- epic itself transitions into done, discarding an override made earlier. A
-- real completion outranks an earlier hand-placement.
--
-- A card with no completed_at at all sorts last, below every dated card, and
-- is refused a manual reorder — there is no value to swap. It is specified
-- because the ordering must be total, not because it is expected: the schema
-- migration dates every pre-existing done row, and every route into done since
-- stamps one, so nothing is left to reach it.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Flattened Mode
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The task board has two rendering modes: the default hierarchical mode
-- and a flattened mode that surfaces every descendant task of the
-- current scope directly into its status column, bypassing the
-- epic/sub-epic grouping. Flattening does not reach every column: the
-- Backlog column is exempt and always renders hierarchically (see
-- Rendering).
--
-- Intent: when work is spread across nested epics, users often need to
-- see at a glance which tasks are running, blocked, awaiting review or
-- already finished, without drilling into each epic individually. The
-- flattened mode answers that need without changing any task data. The
-- exemption is the other half of the same intent: the backlog is the one
-- column read at the epic level rather than task by task — it is where
-- work is planned and ordered, and a flat list of every unstarted task in
-- every epic is not a plan — so flattening it would cost more than it
-- gives.
--
-- Done was exempt too, on the reasoning that completed work is read at the
-- epic level. It is not: "what did we finish" is a question about tasks,
-- and an exempt Done column answered it with an epic card whose only
-- report was a count. Done now flattens with Running and Review.
--
-- Scope: determined by the current view.
--   - Main board view: scope is every task that passes the
--     active repo filter, regardless of epic membership or depth.
--   - Epic view: scope is every task whose epic chain
--     (epic_id, then epic.parent, recursively) roots at the current
--     epic, plus any task directly under it. The repo filter still
--     applies.
--   - Either way the scope is narrowed again per column: only a column
--     that is being flattened shows the whole subtree. An unflattened
--     column shows what hierarchical mode would show there — on the main
--     board, tasks with no epic; in an epic view, the current epic's own
--     direct tasks. See Rendering.
--
-- Rendering:
--   - Flattening is per column, not board-wide. It applies to the Running,
--     Review and Done columns. The Backlog column is never flattened: it
--     renders exactly as it does in hierarchical mode, with epic cards
--     visible and no descendant surfaced out of its epic. On the main board
--     that leaves only standalone tasks (no epic) as task cards there; in an
--     epic view it leaves the current epic's own direct tasks, with deeper
--     descendants still inside their sub-epic's card. It is the one column
--     read at the epic level — the backlog stays navigable at the epic level
--     — which is why it keeps its epic cards while the other three give
--     theirs up.
--   - In a flattened column, task cards appear in their normal visual
--     column, determined by (task.status, task.sub_status), exactly as in
--     hierarchical mode, and no epic card is rendered there — neither
--     root epics on the board nor sub-epics inside an epic view.
--   - Epic membership is carried by the epic-header rule below, not by
--     the task card itself: a flattened task card renders exactly as it
--     does in hierarchical mode, with no epic badge of its own.
--   - The decorative epic-header row that precedes each epic's task group
--     shows the title of the epic the group belongs to, and a muted rule
--     fills the rest of the row to its right: `<title> ───`. It carries no
--     id (board-visuals.allium: EpicsAreShownByTitleNeverById). No rule is
--     drawn to the left of the title, so the row's indent (below) is the
--     first thing it shows.
--   - The row shows that epic's own title and nothing else — not its
--     ancestors. A nested epic's header names only the epic its tasks sit
--     in directly, root or not: the full ancestor path is long and buries
--     the one title that tells the groups apart. Two epics that share a
--     title are told apart by their place in the indented tree. The title
--     is bounded by the column width, less the row's indent — the same
--     reservation task cards and epic cards
--     already make for their lead word before computing their truncation
--     budget — and truncated with an ellipsis when it does not fit.
--   - Nesting is carried by order and indent, not by header text. With
--     own-title-only headers sorted by epic id alone, a sub-epic's group
--     could sit far from its parent's and the two would read as unrelated.
--     So, within each substatus section, the epic groups are laid out as
--     the epic tree (EpicGroupTree below), built from that section's tasks
--     alone: two groups in different sections never share a header.
--   - The tree is compact. It shows where groups meet, not the whole path
--     to the root: the full path to the top is noise when only one group
--     sits under it, and indent for a group with no sibling
--     says nothing.
--       - A header is drawn for an epic that owns a task in the section,
--         and for an epic where two or more drawn branches meet: the
--         nearest parent two groups share. Every other epic is skipped —
--         one above the topmost meeting point, and one that only a single
--         branch passes through. A skipped epic draws no header and adds
--         no indent.
--       - So a section with one epic group, or with groups that share no
--         parent, draws each group's own header flush at depth 0. The
--         header still names the epic its tasks belong to.
--       - An epic drawn only because branches meet has no task of its own
--         in the section. Its header is followed directly by its children.
--       - Groups come in tree order: an epic's group, then the subtrees of
--         the drawn epics directly under it. Inside one epic's group its
--         own tasks come first. Then come the drawn children that have no
--         drawn child of their own, then the drawn children that do: a
--         flush block never sits below a deeper one under the same parent.
--         Each of those two runs keeps the epics' card order (sort_order,
--         else id — the key top-level groups already sorted by). Top-level
--         groups are ordered the same way.
--       - The tree's top: on the main board and in a work-package view,
--         epics with no parent; in an epic view, the viewed epic's direct
--         sub-epics. The viewed epic itself is already named by the border
--         title ("Epic View Panel Title"), so its own direct tasks keep a
--         depth-0 header of their own, first, and its sub-epics are depth 0
--         after it, not under it.
--       - Only epics the column can draw count as ancestors. An ancestor
--         that is out of the view — above the viewed epic, or not on the
--         board at all — is skipped like any other skipped epic, even where
--         branches meet at it. (A hidden epic never arises here: hiding an
--         epic hides its whole subtree.)
--       - Each row of a group — its header and its task cards alike — is
--         indented by `config.epic_indent_step` columns per level of depth,
--         depth counted from 0 at the tree's top and one more per drawn
--         header above the group. The indent stops growing
--         at `config.epic_indent_max_depth`: deeper groups draw at that
--         depth's indent, so a narrow column keeps room for its titles. A
--         card's width shrinks by its indent; the card renders otherwise
--         exactly as it does at depth 0. Cards are indented with their
--         header, not left flush: a header stepped in above flush cards
--         reads as a header that drifted, not as a child group.
--   - Within each substatus group, epic-owned tasks come first and tasks
--     with no epic come last, unindented. A second decorative rule —
--     visually distinct from the epic-header one — separates the two,
--     marking where the epic groups end. It is drawn only at that
--     transition, so a group of all-epic or all-orphan tasks shows none.
--     Like the epic header it is decoration: neither rule is selectable and
--     neither counts towards the column's navigable items.
--   - The flattened Done column has no sections and draws no epic headers
--     (see "Done Column Ordering"), so it has no tree to show: it keeps its
--     freshest-first group order and draws no indent.
--
-- State model:
--   - Flattened is derived from the board lens (BoardLens.flattened): it
--     holds under the `flat` and `flat_active` lenses. See "Board Lens".
--   - The lens is preserved as the user navigates into sub-epics and
--     back out: drilling down or exiting an epic does not change it.
--   - It is session-scoped and does not persist across app restarts.
--
-- Interaction:
--   - Task-card actions (dispatch, go-into-task, expand-description)
--     behave identically in both modes. A flattened task card always
--     acts on the task itself, never on its ancestor epic.
--   - Epic cards stay visible and selectable in Backlog, the one unflattened
--     column, so an epic with unstarted work can be entered without leaving
--     flattened mode. An epic whose subtree holds no visible backlog task
--     draws no card anywhere in flattened mode, because its other columns are
--     all flattened; to navigate into it the user first turns flattened mode
--     off, and every epic card becomes visible and selectable again.


config {
    -- Columns of indent per level of epic depth, for a flattened column's
    -- epic headers and cards and the agent-tree pane's agents section.
    epic_indent_step: Integer = 2
    -- The depth past which the indent stops growing.
    epic_indent_max_depth: Integer = 3
    -- The longest an epic title runs in the epic view's border breadcrumb
    -- before it is truncated with an ellipsis ("Epic View Panel Title").
    breadcrumb_segment_max_chars: Integer = 30
}

-- The compact epic tree one flattened section (or the agent-tree pane's
-- agents section) lays its groups out as. `top` is the viewed epic in an
-- epic view, and null on the main board, in a package view and in the pane:
-- only epics strictly below the top may count as ancestors, so the tree's
-- top is the viewed epic's direct sub-epics or the root epics. `owners` is
-- the set of epics that own a task in the section (an agent in the pane).
-- The viewed epic's own group is still drawn, at depth 0; it is only never
-- an ancestor.
value EpicGroupTree {
    top: core/Epic?
    owners: Set<core/Epic>

    -- Whether an epic may count as an ancestor here.
    in_view(epic): top = null or epic in top.subtree_epics

    -- Whether some owner sits anywhere below the epic.
    has_owner_below(epic): owners.any(o => o in epic.subtree_epics)

    -- How many of the epic's child epics are an owner or have an owner
    -- below them. Counted over its children in the full epic tree, so an
    -- epic only one branch passes through counts 1 however wide the subtree
    -- beneath that branch is.
    branches(epic):
        filter(epic.sub_epics, c => c in owners or has_owner_below(c)).count

    -- An epic draws a header when it owns a task here, or when two or more
    -- branches meet at it in view. Every other epic is skipped. An owner
    -- out of view (only ever the viewed epic) still draws its header.
    shown(epic):
        epic in owners or (in_view(epic) and branches(epic) >= 2)

    -- Whether a drawn epic counts as an ancestor: only one in view.
    counts(epic): in_view(epic) and shown(epic)

    -- The nearest ancestor drawn, skipping every one that does not count.
    -- Null at the tree's top.
    drawn_parent(epic):
        if epic.parent = null: null
        else if counts(epic.parent): epic.parent
        else: drawn_parent(epic.parent)

    -- 0 at the tree's top; one more per counted ancestor.
    depth(epic):
        if drawn_parent(epic) = null: 0
        else: depth(drawn_parent(epic)) + 1

    -- A drawn epic with no drawn epic directly under it. Under one parent
    -- these come before the drawn children that are not leaves.
    is_leaf(epic):
        not epic.subtree_epics.any(e => counts(e) and drawn_parent(e) = epic)

    indent(epic):
        min(depth(epic), config.epic_indent_max_depth) * config.epic_indent_step
}

-- == Epic View Panel Title ==
--
-- Independent of flattened mode: whenever the current view is a specific
-- epic (ViewMode::Epic), the panel enclosing the columns is bordered, and
-- the border's title carries that epic's ancestor breadcrumb — the root
-- ancestor first, the epic itself last, segments joined with " › " and
-- each truncated to config.breadcrumb_segment_max_chars characters. board-visuals.allium's
-- HuedSurface.epic_view_panel_ground documents this same panel's hue; this
-- entry documents what its title says.
--
-- The title carries no id (board-visuals.allium:
-- EpicsAreShownByTitleNeverById). Unlike the flattened epic-header row
-- (see "Flattened Mode"), the title keeps the whole ancestor path: it says
-- where the user is, while the header row only has to tell one task group
-- from the next.

-- == Column Section Layout ==

-- Which columns have sections (BoardView.has_sections), and which section a
-- card lands in (BoardView.section_of). An epic card lands in a section too,
-- so an epic and a task in the same state group together rather than the
-- epic floating above every header; a copy's section is read off its own
-- column's placing tasks ("Epic Card Placement"). An epic in a column with no
-- sections has no section, exactly as a task there has none. The
-- ColumnSection values themselves, including the three derived ones, are in
-- core.allium, "Column Sections".
-- == Collapsed Sections ==

-- A section can be collapsed: its header stays, its cards are hidden, and the
-- header says how many are hidden.
--
-- Intent: a section can grow without bound while the user has no intention of
-- acting on it right now — reviews awaiting a decision from someone else,
-- approvals the user already gave. An unbounded section pushes every other
-- section in the column off the bottom of the screen, so the sections the user
-- IS working on become the hardest ones to see. Collapsing is the user saying
-- "not this pile, not now", without touching a single task.
--
-- Identity: a collapse is recorded per (column, section), so the conflict
-- section in Running and the conflict section in Review collapse
-- independently. Recording it against the section's identity rather than its
-- header text means a section whose contents turn over completely stays
-- collapsed.
--
-- Rendering:
--   - A collapsed section renders its header and nothing else. The header
--     carries the number of cards it is hiding and a marker showing it is
--     folded. An expanded section's header carries neither: its cards are
--     right there to be counted, and the absence of a marker is what makes a
--     collapsed one stand out.
--   - The marker is an ellipsis, not the small triangle the board uses
--     elsewhere. That triangle already carries two unrelated meanings — the
--     focused column and "this epic has a plan" — and a third would make all
--     three ambiguous.
--   - A section header occupies two rows, not one: a blank spacer above the
--     label, which separates it from the section before it. The column's first
--     header drops the spacer, so the top of a column has no leading gap. Both
--     hold whether the section is folded or open, so folding a section changes
--     the rows its cards took and nothing else.
--   - A header holding the cursor is drawn in the cursor's near-white with a
--     neutral fill behind it — the same two neutrals the board's other cursors
--     and its select-all checkbox use, and no hue, so the cursor never
--     competes with the state colours around it. A card shows the cursor on its
--     frame; a header has no frame, and a brightness step alone on one
--     already-bold line is too small to find, so it takes both.
--   - The count is of cards the column would otherwise have rendered there —
--     what the repo filter, the epic hide-list, the only-active filter and the
--     search query all admit. A section is never described as hiding a card
--     the user could not have seen anyway.
--   - A section with no cards renders no header, collapsed or not, exactly as
--     today. Its collapse is remembered regardless, so the header comes back
--     folded when a card next lands in it.
--   - Collapsing applies wherever sections render: the main board, an epic
--     view, and a flattened column alike, all off the same recorded state.
--     In a flattened column the section's epic headers and orphan separator
--     are hidden along with its cards — they are decoration on cards that are
--     not there.
--   - The column's own header count (board-visuals.allium: "Column header bar") is
--     unchanged by folding. It answers how much work is in the column, and
--     folding is a choice about screen space rather than about the work. So a
--     folded card is still counted there, and section headers — which are not
--     cards — are not. The per-section count on a folded header is what says
--     where the uncounted-on-screen cards went.
--   - Split view changes nothing here. It draws the same four columns inside a
--     focus border, so their sections render and fold exactly as they do with
--     the pane closed.
--
-- Interaction:
--   - A collapsed section's header holds the cursor: it is the only way back
--     to a section whose cards are all hidden. An expanded section's header
--     does not, so navigation with nothing collapsed is exactly as it was.
--     A folded section and an open header are therefore two different kinds of
--     item, not one item with a flag: whether a thing can hold the cursor is a
--     property of what it is, so that every reader of a column's contents gets
--     the same answer without re-deriving it.
--   - Folding puts the cursor on the folded section's own header, and
--     unfolding puts it on that section's first card. Either way the cursor
--     stays in the section the user acted on: it never lands on a card that
--     is no longer rendered, and never slides into a neighbouring section
--     because a row index happened to stay in bounds.
--   - Under a live query there is no folded header to move to, since nothing
--     draws folded. Folding then records the fold and leaves the cursor to
--     clamp, and the header appears — with the cursor free to reach it — once
--     the query is cleared.
--   - A folded section whose last card disappears loses its header, and with
--     it the cursor's resting place. The cursor then clamps within the same
--     column, as it does for any card that leaves the board.
--   - Toggling is specified in tasks.allium: ToggleSectionCollapse.
--   - No action other than that toggle does anything with the cursor on a
--     header. There is no task and no epic under it, so move, delete,
--     select, dispatch, edit and open-url all decline rather than reaching
--     for a neighbouring card.
--   - A collapsed section's hidden cards are outside every board action that
--     works on "the column": select-all reaches the cards the column renders,
--     which does not include them. The cards are still selected if they were
--     selected before the collapse — collapsing hides cards, it does not
--     deselect them.
--   - So the focused column's header bar carries two numbers answering
--     different questions, and this is deliberate: the count includes a folded
--     section's cards, because it reports the work in the column, while the
--     select-all checkbox reads "all selected" off the rendered cards only,
--     because that is exactly the set select-all acts on. A checkbox that
--     waited on cards the user cannot see could not be satisfied without
--     unfolding first.
--
-- Search override:
--   - While a search query is active and some card the query admits falls in a
--     collapsed section, that section renders expanded. A search that hid its
--     own results would be worse than no search.
--   - The override is display-only: the recorded collapse is untouched, so
--     clearing the query folds the section again.
--
-- State model:
--   - The set of collapsed (column, section) pairs is user state and persists
--     across app restarts. "Not this pile, not now" outlives a session in a
--     way the flattened toggle does not.
--   - A recorded pair for a section that no longer exists is inert: it names
--     nothing the board renders, and it neither errors nor collapses anything
--     else.

-- One folded section, named by the column it is in and the section within it.
-- The same section name in two columns is two independent pairs.
value SectionRef {
    column: core/TaskStatus
    section: core/ColumnSection
}

-- An entity rather than a value, on the same terms as core.allium's
-- BoardSelection:
-- the rule that folds and unfolds a section mutates it, so it has to be
-- something a rule elsewhere can be given. There is exactly one.
entity CollapsedSections {
    collapsed: Set<SectionRef>

    is_collapsed(ref): ref in collapsed

    -- What the user sees, as opposed to what is recorded: while a query is
    -- live, no section draws folded.
    --
    -- Stated that way rather than as "a folded section holding a match is
    -- forced open", which is the intent, because the two are the same rule and
    -- this one needs no per-section card count to evaluate. A section the query
    -- leaves empty renders no header at all, folded or not (see Rendering
    -- above), so overriding it is unobservable.
    renders_collapsed(ref, search_query):
        is_collapsed(ref) and search_query = ""

    -- A header holds the cursor only while it draws folded. An expanded one
    -- does not, and neither does a section that renders no header.
    header_is_selectable(ref, search_query):
        renders_collapsed(ref, search_query)

    @guidance
        -- Persisted, unlike the lens and BoardSelection: it survives an
        -- app restart.
}

-- == Epic Folding ==

-- A flattened epic's group can be folded too, independently of section
-- folding: its header stays, its cards are hidden, and the header says how
-- many are hidden. Same shape as Collapsed Sections, over a different axis
-- of the same column.
--
-- Intent: the same "not this pile, not now" Collapsed Sections gives a
-- substatus, cast on epic membership instead. An epic whose flattened work
-- has spread wide crowds a column the same way an unbounded section does,
-- and the user's fix is the same kind of move: set the whole group aside
-- without touching a task.
--
-- Applies only where the flattened epic-header row renders at all (see
-- "Flattened Mode", the decorative epic-header rule under "Rendering"): a
-- column being flattened, in a section holding that epic's tasks. Nowhere
-- else names an epic group to fold — an unflattened column has no such row
-- (what it shows instead are real epic cards, a different concept this
-- feature does not touch), and neither does a flattened column with no
-- header for that epic to begin with. Today that is Done: it groups a
-- flattened epic's tasks by order alone and draws no header for the group
-- (see "Done Column Ordering"), so there is nothing there to fold. Reusing
-- BoardView.draws_epic_headers rather than a fresh per-column check keeps this
-- from drifting the day flattening's own per-column boundary changes.
--
-- Identity: a fold is recorded per (column, epic), not per (column,
-- section, epic). A single epic's flattened tasks in one column can
-- straddle more than one substatus section — some needing input, some
-- plain running — and folding the epic hides all of them at once, in every
-- section of that column. The same epic folds independently in each column
-- it appears in, on the same reasoning epic card placement already gives it
-- one independent card, and one independent cursor position, per column
-- (see "Epic Card Placement").
--
-- Independent of Collapsed Sections: an epic fold and a section fold are
-- two separate recorded sets over possibly-overlapping cards, and neither
-- rule reads the other's state. A folded section hides its epic groups
-- along with everything else in the run — folded or not, there is nothing
-- left in that section to render, so an epic fold inside it has nothing to
-- show until the section itself reopens. Once the section is open, a
-- folded epic within it renders its own folded header exactly as an
-- unfolded epic renders its group, unaffected by the section fold either
-- way.
--
-- Rendering, interaction and the search override are the same shape as a
-- collapsed section's, over an epic group instead of a substatus run:
--   - A folded epic group renders its header and nothing else: no task
--     cards. The header itself is unchanged otherwise — it still carries
--     the epic's own title, exactly as the open header
--     does, so the user can tell which epic a folded header names without
--     unfolding it first — and gains the hidden count and the folded
--     marker, which the open header carries neither of. Same relationship
--     as a collapsed section's header to its open one: folding adds to what
--     the header says, never replaces it.
--   - The header holds the cursor only while it draws folded, the only way
--     back to a group whose cards are all hidden. An open epic-header row
--     is decoration, exactly as it is today, and does not.
--   - Folding puts the cursor on the group's own folded header; unfolding
--     puts it on the group's first card — for a parent with no card of its
--     own in the column, the first row of its subtree that holds the
--     cursor: a card, or a folded descendant's header, whichever is drawn
--     first. No action but the toggle does
--     anything with the cursor on a folded header — see Collapsed
--     Sections's identical rule for why.
--   - While a search query is active and some card it admits sits in a
--     folded epic's group, that group renders unfolded, on the same
--     reasoning as the section override: a search that hid its own results
--     would be worse than no search. The override is display-only; the
--     recorded fold is untouched, and clearing the query folds the group
--     again.
--   - A folded group whose last card disappears loses its header, and the
--     cursor clamps within the same column, as it does for any card that
--     leaves the board.
--   - Nested groups (see "Flattened Mode", EpicGroupTree): a folded epic hides
--     its whole subtree in that section — its own cards and every
--     descendant's header and cards — and its hidden count is the subtree's
--     tasks there. A folded header keeps its indent and, like the open one,
--     has no rule to the left of its id. Folding a parent does not touch a
--     descendant's recorded fold: when the parent unfolds, each descendant
--     draws folded or open exactly as it was recorded before. A folded
--     header directly under its parent's open header draws no blank line
--     above it: the two belong together. An open parent header with no
--     task of its own in the column cannot be folded: no card there
--     belongs to it, and the open header is decoration (tasks.allium:
--     ToggleEpicFold).
--   - The compact tree (EpicGroupTree) is built from every task in the
--     section, folded or not: folding changes what a drawn header hides,
--     never which headers are drawn. A folded epic with no task of its own
--     keeps its header while branches still meet at it.
--   - A fold never hides a task without a header above it. In a section
--     where a folded epic is skipped, its fold hides nothing there: the
--     tasks below it draw under their own headers.
--   - When a folded epic is drawn in no section of its column while the
--     column still holds tasks below it, the fold moves down
--     (tasks.allium: FoldMovesToDrawnDescendant). Each drawn epic that
--     takes its place — the drawn epic nearest below it, in each section
--     where it is skipped — is folded, and the skipped epic's fold is
--     cleared. The
--     user folded it to hide what sits under it, and what still sits there
--     is now headed by those epics. When the skipped epic is drawn again
--     later, it draws open.
--   - The move judges the column unfiltered: every task the column holds
--     on the main board, as if no search, lens narrowing, hidden epic,
--     hidden work package or repo filter were on. Those narrow the drawn
--     tree for a moment and can skip an epic only while they last; a fold
--     the user recorded must not move, and be saved moved, because of
--     one. While they last, a folded epic they skip hides nothing, as in
--     any section that skips it.
--
-- Toggling is specified in tasks.allium: ToggleEpicFold.
--
-- State model:
--   - The set of folded (column, epic) pairs is user state and persists
--     across app restarts, on the same reasoning as Collapsed Sections:
--     "not this epic, not now" outlives a session.
--   - A recorded pair for an epic that no longer places a flattened group in
--     that column is inert: it names nothing the column renders, and it
--     neither errors nor folds anything else.

-- One folded epic group, named by the column it is in and the epic within
-- it. The same epic in two columns is two independent pairs.
value EpicFoldRef {
    column: core/TaskStatus
    epic: core/Epic
}

-- An entity rather than a value, on the same terms as CollapsedSections: the
-- rule that folds and unfolds an epic group mutates it. There is exactly
-- one.
entity FoldedEpics {
    folded: Set<EpicFoldRef>

    is_folded(ref): ref in folded

    -- What the user sees, as opposed to what is recorded: while a query is
    -- live, no epic group draws folded. Same reasoning as
    -- CollapsedSections.renders_collapsed.
    renders_folded(ref, search_query):
        is_folded(ref) and search_query = ""

    -- A header holds the cursor only while it draws folded.
    header_is_selectable(ref, search_query):
        renders_folded(ref, search_query)

    @guidance
        -- Persisted, like CollapsedSections and unlike the lens and
        -- BoardSelection: it survives an app restart.
}

-- == Board Vertical Layout ==

-- board_vertical_layout:
--   The board frame is split top to bottom into five bands: the top
--   indicator bar, the summary row, the kanban board (columns), the input
--   panel, and the status bar.
--
--   The indicator bar, summary row and status bar are each exactly one row.
--   The kanban board and the input panel share whatever vertical space is
--   left between them, and that split is governed by the input panel's mode:
--
--   Idle: the input panel's height is zero whenever the current input mode
--   has no form to show in that panel. It draws nothing — no border, no
--   background — and the kanban board claims the full remaining height, so
--   columns run uninterrupted down to the status bar. This is the default
--   (no input mode active) but is not limited to it: a mode whose prompt
--   is shown elsewhere — a y/n confirmation in the status bar, the live
--   search bar, the help overlay, the board-filter popup, an epic/task
--   picker overlay — is idle from the panel's perspective too, since it has
--   nothing of its own to draw there.
--
--   Active: a mode whose form the panel itself draws (new task, new epic,
--   quick dispatch, retry confirmation, and so on) reserves enough rows to
--   show that form, bordered and titled for the mode. Most such modes need
--   a fixed number of rows; a mode that lists repositories (quick dispatch,
--   or the repo-path prompt with an empty query) grows the panel to fit
--   every matching repo instead, up to the space the board can spare — the
--   kanban board never drops below a minimum height to make room for it.
--
--   Entering or leaving an input mode changes the panel's height
--   immediately; the kanban board resizes on the same frame, never leaving
--   a gap or an overlap between the two bands.

-- == Board Lens ==

-- The board has two independent controls over what it shows:
--   - The LENS says HOW the board is read: every card, or only the cards with
--     a live session; grouped under epics, or flattened. Only-active always
--     comes with flattening (see "Dropped lens" below). One key cycles it.
--   - The board FILTER says WHICH work is on the board at all: repos, epics
--     and work packages. It is set in the board-filter overlay (`f`, below).
--
-- Intent: one key per reading mode would grow a board key for every new axis
-- of "which things". Splitting the two leaves one key for the reading mode
-- and puts every "which things" choice in one overlay, where a new axis is a
-- new tab rather than a new board key. Only-active is heavily used and stays
-- on the board, not in the overlay: it is a lens. Tab steps the lens forward
-- and BackTab (Shift+Tab) steps it back, so every lens is at most two presses
-- away. There are no `A` and `F` toggle keys.
--
-- The three lenses, in cycle order:
--   everything   → every card the board filter admits (the default)
--   flat         → the flattened rendering (see "Flattened Mode")
--   flat_active  → flattened, and only tasks with a live session
--                  (tmux_window set)
-- Forward wraps from flat_active to everything; backward wraps the other way.
--
-- Dropped lens: there is no `active` lens, only-active with the columns still
-- grouped under epics. The operator does not use only-active without
-- flattening, so it would cost a Tab press on every cycle for nothing.
--
-- The lens reaches inside a package view as it does everywhere else: both
-- halves apply there (work-packages.allium: PackageViewCards).
--
-- What `flat_active` narrows: only tasks where tmux_window is set are
-- visible (BoardView.task_is_visible), in addition to the board filter. It
-- is called the only-active filter everywhere else in this file:
-- BoardLens.only_active is that predicate's switch.
--
-- State model:
--   - One lens per board, preserved as the user navigates into sub-epics and
--     back out.
--   - Session-scoped: every app start begins at `everything`.
--   - The lens shows as the status bar's existing badges: `[flat]` for
--     flat, both `[active]` and `[flat]` for flat_active. `everything` shows
--     neither.
--
-- No MCP tool reads or sets the lens. It is the operator's reading of their
-- own board for one session, needs nothing from the shared store (store-split
-- S1 keeps it in the TUI), and no agent sees that board.

enum Lens { everything | flat | flat_active }

entity BoardLens {
    lens: Lens

    -- The two switches the rest of this file reads. Derived, never set on
    -- their own: the lens is the only state.
    only_active: lens = flat_active
    flattened: lens = flat or lens = flat_active

    next: if lens = everything: flat
          else if lens = flat: flat_active
          else: everything

    previous: if lens = everything: flat_active
              else if lens = flat: everything
              else: flat
}

rule CycleLensForward {
    when: CycleLensForward()
    ensures: board_view.lens.lens = board_view.lens.next

    @guidance
        -- Bound to Tab on the board (board.normal), in the main board and in
        -- an epic view alike.
}

rule CycleLensBackward {
    when: CycleLensBackward()
    ensures: board_view.lens.lens = board_view.lens.previous

    @guidance
        -- Bound to BackTab (Shift+Tab) on the board (board.normal).
}

-- == Hidden Epics and Work Packages ==

-- Besides the repo filter, the board filter holds two hide-lists: epics the
-- user has hidden and work packages the user has hidden. Both are lists of
-- what is HIDDEN, never of what is shown: an epic or package created after
-- the user hid something else is visible. A filter that listed what to show
-- would make new work vanish silently on a board agents add work to.
--
-- Hiding an epic hides its whole subtree:
--   - The epic's own card, and the cards of every descendant sub-epic.
--   - Every task in its subtree, in every column, in hierarchical and
--     flattened lenses alike. Flattening would otherwise bring the hidden
--     work back as plain task cards.
--   - A task in the subtree is hidden even when a visible work package links
--     it directly: the hide is the stronger statement.
--   - Inside an epic view the hides apply too: a hidden sub-epic draws no
--     card inside its parent's view. Entering a hidden epic is not possible
--     from the board, because its card is gone. When the overlay closes while
--     the user stands inside an epic or package view they have just hidden
--     (the epic itself or an ancestor), the board climbs out one level at a
--     time until the view it shows is not hidden, so it never sits inside
--     work it no longer shows.
--   - The move-to-epic and reparent pickers still offer hidden epics: moving
--     work into one is an explicit choice, and the hide only narrows what
--     the board shows.
--
-- Hiding a work package hides its card and nothing else. A package is a view
-- over work, not work; its linked tasks and epics stay on the board.
--
-- Hidden work is invisible in the sense every visibility clause in this file
-- uses: a task under a hidden epic cannot place an ancestor's epic card
-- (BoardView.placing_tasks), keep an epic card alive under a search
-- (board_search_filter), or keep a work package's card alive under a search
-- (work-packages.allium: PackageCardPlacement). It still counts toward which
-- column a package card sits in: no filter moves a package card.
--
-- State model:
--   - Persisted, like the repo selection and its include/exclude mode: both
--     hide-lists are saved as this host's settings when the overlay closes
--     (SaveBoardFilterOnClose), and read back at startup. The
--     whole board filter therefore survives a restart; only the lens does
--     not. Re-hiding the same epics after every restart would be the cost of
--     the alternative, and the badge below keeps a forgotten hide visible.
--   - An id on a hide-list whose epic or package no longer exists is inert:
--     it hides nothing and errors on nothing. Nothing prunes it, but nothing
--     counts it either: the badge below and the overlay's tab counts name
--     only hides of epics and packages the board holds, since the user could
--     never find the row to unhide a ghost.
--   - While anything is hidden, the status bar shows a badge naming the
--     counts, e.g. `[hidden: 2 epics, 1 package]`, so a forgotten hide does
--     not read as missing work. Nothing hidden, no badge.
--
-- No MCP tool reads or sets the hide-lists: they are the operator's view of
-- their own board, and no agent sees that board. The saved settings are
-- per-host rows like the repo filter's, not shared data about the work.
--
-- The one other reader is the operator's own view beside an agent: the
-- agent-tree pane's agents section lists only the agents this filter shows
-- (agent-tree.allium: ListsFollowTheBoardFilter). The board applies the
-- epic hide-list and the repo filter there, not the search, the lens or the
-- package hide-list, and it reads them from the saved settings, so a change
-- reaches the pane once the overlay closes.

entity BoardHides {
    hidden_epics: Set<core/Epic>
    hidden_packages: Set<wp/WorkPackage>

    -- An epic is hidden when it or any ancestor is on the list.
    epic_is_hidden(epic):
        epic in hidden_epics
        or hidden_epics.any(h => epic in h.subtree_epics)

    -- A task is hidden when its epic is. A task with no epic is never hidden
    -- by this list.
    task_is_hidden(task):
        task.epic != null and epic_is_hidden(task.epic)

    -- A shown epic with some sub-epic on the list: part of its subtree is
    -- hidden though its own card is not. The overlay marks it partly hidden.
    epic_is_partly_hidden(epic):
        not epic_is_hidden(epic)
        and hidden_epics.any(h => h in epic.subtree_epics)

    package_is_hidden(package): package in hidden_packages

    @guidance
        -- Each list is saved as ids, so it can name an epic or package the
        -- board no longer holds. Such an entry hides nothing; the counts
        -- the badge shows are BoardView.hidden_epic_count and
        -- hidden_package_count, which skip it.
}

rule ToggleHiddenEpic {
    when: ToggleHiddenEpic(epic)
    ensures:
        if epic in board_view.hides.hidden_epics
            or board_view.hides.epic_is_partly_hidden(epic):
            -- Show the whole subtree: the epic and every sub-epic leave the
            -- list, done ones and unlisted ones included.
            board_view.hides.hidden_epics.remove(epic)
            for sub_epic in epic.subtree_epics:
                board_view.hides.hidden_epics.remove(sub_epic)
        else:
            board_view.hides.hidden_epics.add(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Space on an epic row in the overlay's Epics tab. The tab works like
        -- a tree of checkboxes: hiding a parent hides its subtree, and showing
        -- it again shows the whole subtree, clearing any sub-epic the user had
        -- hidden on its own. Space on a partly hidden row shows
        -- its whole subtree too; a second Space then hides the parent. Space
        -- on a row hidden by its parent adds or removes that epic's own entry
        -- (removing also clears its sub-epics' entries), which changes nothing
        -- visible while the parent stays hidden.
}

rule ToggleHiddenWorkPackage {
    when: ToggleHiddenWorkPackage(package)
    ensures:
        if package in board_view.hides.hidden_packages:
            board_view.hides.hidden_packages.remove(package)
        else:
            board_view.hides.hidden_packages.add(package)

    @guidance
        -- Space on a package row in the overlay's Work packages tab.
}

rule SaveBoardFilterOnClose {
    when: CloseBoardFilter()
    ensures:
        settings/SaveSetting(key: "hidden_epics", value: id_list(board_view.hides.hidden_epics))
        settings/SaveSetting(key: "hidden_packages", value: id_list(board_view.hides.hidden_packages))

    @guidance
        -- Closing the [f] overlay saves the whole board filter, once, rather
        -- than one write per Space. The same close saves the repo selection
        -- (key "repo_filter") and its include/exclude mode
        -- ("repo_filter_mode"); this spec does not model those as state, so
        -- they are named here rather than in the ensures.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- id_list(set) is the ids in the set, sorted, as the setting's
        -- stored text.
}

-- == Board Filter Overlay Layout ==

-- board_filter_overlay_layout:
--   The [f] overlay is a centred popup with three tabs, in this order:
--   Repos, Epics, Work packages. It opens on Repos. Exactly one tab shows at
--   a time; its name is highlighted in a tab-header row at the top of the
--   popup. Tab moves to the next tab and BackTab (Shift+Tab) to the previous,
--   both wrapping.
--
--   Tabs, not one list: a live board holds about 150 epics, nested up to five
--   levels, against a handful of packages and a few dozen repos. One
--   scrolling list would bury the packages under the epics.
--
--   Content rows, top to bottom: the tab header, the active tab's list, then
--   a footer block of help rows. There is no preset section, no preset-name
--   input row, and no delete-preset confirm: there are no saved filter
--   presets. `s`, `x` and the capital letters other than `Z` are ignored in
--   the overlay.
--
--   Each tab keeps its own cursor and its own scroll, so switching away and
--   back returns to the same row. Space toggles the row under the cursor in
--   every tab. There is no toggle-all key: `a` is ignored in the overlay.
--
--   Repos tab: the repo list, as before. Space includes or excludes the repo
--   under the cursor; `1`-`9` toggle the repo at that list position
--   regardless of the cursor; `i` switches the whole repo filter between
--   including and excluding the toggled repos; Backspace/Delete forgets the repo path, after a
--   confirm, which takes the footer down to one row. The Repos footer shows
--   `[Del] forget` so the key can be found; it also works on a path that no
--   longer exists on disk, which is what it is for. `1`-`9`, `i` and
--   Backspace/Delete do nothing on the other tabs.
--
--   Epics tab: the epics as a tree, children indented under their parent,
--   each row marked hidden or shown. It lists every epic that is not done,
--   plus every epic on the hide-list itself whatever its status, so a
--   hidden epic that has since finished can still be shown again. (A done
--   epic hidden only through its parent is not listed without `d`: Space on
--   it could not show it anyway.) A listed epic's ancestors are listed too,
--   so no row appears out of its place in the tree. `d` switches the list to every
--   epic, done ones included, and back; this lets the user hide a done
--   epic's cards in the Done column. Each row carries one of four marks:
--   `[ ]` shown, `[x]` hidden on its own, `[-]` hidden by its parent, and
--   `[~]` partly hidden (BoardHides.epic_is_partly_hidden: shown, but some
--   sub-epic is on the hide-list). A child of a hidden parent draws as
--   hidden-by-parent, distinct from hidden on its own, because Space on it
--   cannot bring it back while the parent is hidden; `[-]` wins even when
--   the child is on the hide-list itself. The partly hidden mark
--   is read from the whole subtree, not from the listed rows, so a folded
--   parent still shows that work under it is hidden: without it a folded
--   `[ ]` would read as nothing hidden.
--
--   The tree folds. An epic with listed children carries a `▾` (open) or `▸`
--   (folded) before its checkbox; a leaf carries neither. `z` folds or
--   unfolds the row under the cursor, hiding or showing its whole subtree;
--   `z` on a leaf does nothing. `Z` folds every epic that has children, or,
--   when any is already folded, unfolds them all. Every epic starts folded,
--   so the tab opens on the top-level epics alone: with about 150 epics
--   nested up to five levels, an open tree is hard to move through. An epic that arrives while the overlay is open starts folded
--   too. The first `Z` therefore unfolds everything. The cursor stays on
--   the row it was on, and a fold never changes whether an epic is hidden:
--   a folded hidden parent still hides its children. Folds are the
--   overlay's own: reopening it folds every epic again. While a title
--   query stands, folds are ignored so a match is never tucked away; the
--   tree folds again when the query is cleared. `z` and `Z` do nothing on
--   the other tabs.
--
--   `/` on the Epics tab opens a title search inside the tab: the tree
--   narrows to epics whose title matches (the same fuzzy subsequence match as
--   board_search_filter's title match) and their ancestors, so a match is
--   never shown out of its place in the tree. The query takes its own
--   content row under the tab header while it is non-empty or being typed.
--   Esc with a query clears the query; Esc with none closes the overlay.
--   The query is the overlay's own: it does not touch the board's `/`
--   search, and it is gone when the overlay closes.
--
--   Work packages tab: every work package, one row each, marked hidden or
--   shown.
--
--   Invariant: the footer is never clipped. Every row the overlay draws is
--   reserved by the height budget that sized the popup, including the tab
--   header, the search row when it shows, and the "↑ N more" / "↓ N more"
--   markers that bracket a scrolling list. The markers are content rows, not
--   free decoration: they are paid for out of the same budget as the list
--   rows, so a scrolling list shows fewer rows rather than pushing the help
--   rows off the bottom of the box.
--
--   Sizing the marker rows is circular — whether a marker appears depends on
--   the scroll offset, which depends on how many rows are visible, which
--   depends on how many marker rows were reserved. It is settled as a fixed
--   point over the reservation, smallest first: the overlay reserves the
--   fewest marker rows (0, then 1, then 2) whose resulting window draws no
--   more markers than were reserved. Reserving both always satisfies that, so
--   the settle terminates; preferring smaller reservations means that at
--   either end of a long list, where only one marker is drawn, the spare row
--   goes to a list row instead of staying blank.
--
--   The popup's height is sized by the most rows any tab could list — every
--   repo, every epic (done ones included), every package — up to the
--   board's ceiling, so it does not jump when the user switches tabs,
--   presses `d` or types a query. The one exception is the Epics tab's
--   search row, which adds a row while it shows.
--
--   On a board so short that the popup's chrome alone fills the content area,
--   the list keeps a one-row floor and the markers are dropped: the row
--   under the cursor is worth more than a count of the rows that did not fit.

-- == Board Search Filter ==

-- board_search_filter:
--   When BoardView.search_query is non-empty, the query narrows BOTH task cards and
--   epic cards. An empty query matches everything. This filter is applied in
--   addition to the repo filter, the epic hide-list and the only-active
--   filter (logical AND). A hidden epic's card stays hidden however well it
--   matches: the hide is the user's explicit word, the query is not.
--
--   own_match(entity) — the shared per-entity predicate. It holds when EITHER
--   sub-predicate holds (logical OR):
--     title match: a case-insensitive forward subsequence of entity.title (every
--       query char appears in the title, in order) — the same predicate used by
--       the repo-path picker (fuzzy_matches).
--     id-prefix match: the query, after stripping one optional leading '#', is
--       non-empty and consists entirely of ASCII digits, and the decimal
--       spelling of entity.id starts with those digits. So "38" matches #38,
--       #380 and #3837 (progressive narrowing as the user types) but not
--       #1385; "#3837" matches #3837; "3a" and a bare "#" have no digit
--       payload and so fall back to title matching alone.
--
--   Tasks: a task is visible (BoardView.task_is_visible) only when
--   own_match(task) holds. This applies in the board view and inside an epic view alike: an
--   epic that was surfaced by its own title still shows only those subtasks
--   that match the query, which may be none. Clearing the query is how the
--   user sees an epic's full contents.
--
--   Epics: an epic card is visible when own_match(epic) holds, OR when some
--   descendant sub-epic satisfies own_match AND is itself visible under the
--   repo filter, the epic hide-list and the only-active filter, OR when some
--   task in the epic's subtree satisfies own_match AND is itself visible
--   under the repo filter, the epic hide-list and the only-active filter
--   (BoardView.epic_matches_search; a task is held to the same predicates
--   BoardView.task_is_visible applies, a sub-epic to BoardView.epic_admitted).
--
--   The "itself visible" clause on both the sub-epic and the task branch is
--   load-bearing: a descendant the board would hide cannot keep its ancestor's
--   card alive, because drilling into that card would be a dead end — the
--   descendant that justified the card is not there to be found. The
--   descendant clause exists at all because a hierarchical board does not
--   render epic-owned tasks on the board itself — the epic card is the only
--   path to them, so hiding the card would make a reachable match
--   unreachable; gating the descendant by those three predicates
--   keeps that reachability promise honest.
--
--   Epic ids and task ids are separate sequences, so one digit payload can match
--   both an epic card and a task card. Both are shown.
--
--   Epic-target pickers (reparent, move-task-to-epic) are NOT narrowed by the
--   search query — they carry their own input buffers. See the guidance on
--   ReparentEpic in epics.allium and MoveTaskToEpic in tasks.allium.
--
--   Lifecycle: the [/] key opens a live search bar and the board narrows as
--   the user types. [Enter] keeps the query active and closes the bar (the
--   filter persists, with a [/query] indicator shown on the board). [Esc]
--   in the bar restores the query that was active before the bar opened.
--   [Esc] on the board (bar closed) with an active query clears it.
--   The query is session-scoped and does not persist across app restarts.
--   Cursor re-clamping on query change is covered by selection_preservation.

-- == Cursor Selection Preservation ==

-- selection_preservation:
--   When the cursor has an anchor (a task or epic ID) and column items are recomputed
--   (on any status change, task movement, filter change, or data refresh), the cursor
--   position is resolved by locating the anchor item in the new item list.
--
--   If the anchor item is found: the cursor moves to the item's new column and row,
--   following the item if it changed columns.
--
--   If the anchor item is not present (deleted or moved outside board scope): the cursor
--   clamps to the nearest valid position in the current column.
--
--   When the cursor is on the select-all column header (on_select_all = true), the anchor
--   is None and the header state is preserved across refreshes.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The board's lens keys. Each key's binding is in keybindings.allium.
surface BoardLensKeys {
    facing _: core/Operator

    exposes:
        board_view.lens.lens

    provides:
        -- Tab on the board, in the main board and an epic view alike
        -- (CycleLensForward).
        CycleLensForward()
        -- BackTab (Shift+Tab) on the board (CycleLensBackward).
        CycleLensBackward()
}

-- The [f] board-filter overlay ("Board Filter Overlay Layout").
surface BoardFilterOverlay {
    facing _: core/Operator

    exposes:
        for epic in board_view.epics:
            board_view.hides.epic_is_hidden(epic)
            board_view.hides.epic_is_partly_hidden(epic)
        for package in board_view.packages:
            board_view.hides.package_is_hidden(package)

    provides:
        -- Space on an epic row in the Epics tab (ToggleHiddenEpic).
        ToggleHiddenEpic(epic)
        -- Space on a package row in the Work packages tab
        -- (ToggleHiddenWorkPackage).
        ToggleHiddenWorkPackage(package)
        -- Enter or q, or Esc with no epic title query, closing the overlay
        -- (SaveBoardFilterOnClose).
        CloseBoardFilter()
}

-- The status bar's view badges.
surface BoardViewBadges {
    facing _: core/Operator

    exposes:
        -- `[flat]` under either flattened lens, and `[active]` as well under
        -- flat_active ("Board Lens").
        board_view.lens.flattened
        board_view.lens.only_active
        -- `[hidden: N epics, M packages]` while either count is non-zero
        -- ("Hidden Epics and Work Packages").
        board_view.hidden_epic_count
        board_view.hidden_package_count
        -- `[/query]` while a search query is active ("Board Search Filter").
        board_view.search_query
}

surface TextInputField {
    facing user: core/Operator

    -- The shared single-line text buffer used by every InputMode that
    -- types free text into the app's shared input field: task title,
    -- epic title, base branch, repo-path query, and quick-dispatch query. Task/epic
    -- DESCRIPTION is NOT this surface (it opens an external $EDITOR).
    -- SearchTasks uses a separate buffer and is intentionally not on this
    -- surface yet.

    -- buffer is the text; caret is a CHARACTER index into buffer (the
    -- text insertion point). Both start empty/zero.
    let buffer = ""
    let caret = 0

    exposes:
        buffer
        caret

    provides:
        -- Insert char AT THE CARET (not appended); caret advances by one.
        -- ensures: buffer = insert_char(buffer, caret, char); caret = caret + 1
        TypeChar(user, char)

        -- Delete the char immediately before the caret; caret moves back
        -- one. No-op at caret 0 (guarded).
        -- ensures: buffer = delete_before(buffer, caret); caret = caret - 1
        Backspace(user)
            when caret > 0

        -- Delete key: delete the char AT the caret; caret unchanged.
        -- No-op at end of buffer (guarded).
        -- ensures: buffer = delete_at(buffer, caret)
        DeleteForward(user)
            when caret < len(buffer)

        -- Move the caret one char left, clamped to 0.
        -- ensures: caret = max(0, caret - 1)
        CursorLeft(user)

        -- Move the caret one char right, clamped to len(buffer).
        -- ensures: caret = min(len(buffer), caret + 1)
        CursorRight(user)

        -- Move the caret one word left. A word char is alphanumeric or
        -- underscore; every other char (whitespace, punctuation, path
        -- separators like / - .) is a boundary, so this steps through
        -- path segments.
        -- ensures: caret = word_boundary_left(buffer, caret)
        CursorWordLeft(user)

        -- Move the caret one word right (same word/boundary rule).
        -- ensures: caret = word_boundary_right(buffer, caret)
        CursorWordRight(user)

        -- Jump the caret to the start of the buffer.
        -- ensures: caret = 0
        CursorHome(user)

        -- Jump the caret to the end of the buffer.
        -- ensures: caret = len(buffer)
        CursorEnd(user)

    @guarantee CaretInBounds
        -- caret is always within 0..=len(buffer) after any operation.
        -- Every caret motion clamps to this range and every buffer edit
        -- keeps the caret inside the (possibly resized) buffer.

    @guarantee PrefillLandsAtEnd
        -- When a field is opened prefilled with an existing value
        -- (copy-task repo path, default base branch), the caret starts at len(buffer) — the end — not at 0.

    @guarantee CaretMoveRepaints
        -- A caret motion that changes caret is an observable state change
        -- and must force a redraw even though buffer is unchanged.
        -- Otherwise the rendered caret would not visibly move.

    @guarantee WordMotionWhitespaceAndPunctuation
        -- Word motion breaks on whitespace AND punctuation/path
        -- separators (word char = alphanumeric or `_`). A word jump in a
        -- path with no spaces moves segment-by-segment; in a space-less
        -- token it moves to the boundary. Deliberate — matches
        -- readline/editor default behaviour.

    @guidance
        -- Key bindings: Left/Right move the caret one char; Ctrl+Left/Right
        -- move one word (with Alt+Left/Right and the readline Alt+B / Alt+F
        -- as tmux-safe fallbacks, since some terminals drop the Ctrl
        -- modifier); Home/End jump to the buffer ends; Delete forward-deletes.
        -- Typing and Backspace act AT THE CARET, not at the end of the buffer.
        -- The field is rendered with a reversed block cell at the caret and
        -- horizontal scrolling that keeps the caret visible when the buffer
        -- is wider than the field.
        -- The caret is a Unicode scalar (char) index, so a move or delete can
        -- split a grapheme cluster (combining marks, ZWJ emoji sequences) but
        -- never a UTF-8 codepoint. Grapheme-aware motion is out of scope here.
        -- The insert/delete/word-boundary mechanics (insert_char,
        -- delete_before, delete_at, word_boundary_left/right) are shared UI
        -- helpers documented in docs/conventions.md, not domain logic.
}
